panel has given permission for prosecutors to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:04.680
that obstruction charge, Weissman says that&nbsp;
Jack Smith could now, with that permission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:12.780
File those same charges potentially against Donald&nbsp;
Trump. Let me read you what Weissman had to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:20.700
during an appearance on MSNBC's Morning Joe. This&nbsp;
is really a gift for Jack Smith, because as good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.620
as it is for the Washington, D.C., prosecutors&nbsp;
who have hundreds of pending cases who want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.660
to know that this is a charge they can continue&nbsp;
charging, Jack Smith is going to be thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:36.360
this obstruction charge with respect to the former&nbsp;
president. There's nothing better for Jack Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:42.780
than to know that this has already been approved&nbsp;
by the D.C. Circuit, meaning he can charge it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:48.780
And unless the Supreme Court disagrees with the&nbsp;
D.C. Circuit, he knows that this is a rock solid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:54.900
legally a legal rock solid charge, that he has&nbsp;
to prove it factually. But getting the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.300
pre-clearance is unusual to have, and he has to be&nbsp;
happy that the court ruled that this is a charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.280
that will stick for any potential charge. He is&nbsp;
thinking with respect to the former president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:13.020
A lot to take in, right? Let me distill that&nbsp;
down to its most basic components. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:18.540
the first thing you have is the United States&nbsp;
Supreme Court. It's likely this will obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

